{"format_version":"vbulletin-3+-thread","url":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/mage-the-ascension/1553184-flavors-of-resonance","id":1553184,"title":"Flavors of Resonance","path":["Home","Onyx Path","White Wolf Worlds","World of Darkness","Mage: The Ascension"],"posts":[{"date":"2025-08-14T13:16:46-07:00","id":1553184,"title":"Flavors of Resonance","content":"Instead of making things simpler, Book of Secrets, once again, made them very confusing. First, I'd like to ask if and how you use Resonance in your tables.<br /> <br /> Also, how do you use the &quot;flavors&quot; and how many there are? BoS system is very clear that there are four: Devotional, Elemental, Stabilizing and Temperamental. But then it also have a LOT of random examples like Sorrowful, Joyous, Warlike, Depressing, Tempestuous, Calm, Devotional, Savage, Tragic, Malicious, Treacherous, Feral, Prosperous, Greedy, Accultured, Honorable, Sneaky, Sinister, Corrupting, and the list goes on and on...<br /> <br /> So, do we have to fit those descriptions into the four main flavors? What if they fit into more than one (or none at all)? By reading the Four Flavors of Resonance (p. 136) it seems so, but where does Warlike fits in?<br /> <br /> None of that would matter if this trait was only for fluff (which they imply it is), but then the books add rules that depends on it, like modifiers for casting spells and crafting wonders with them.<br /> <br /> So, how can one say if the flavor is opposed or similar? If a Malicious resonance mage casts a fireball into someone is it appropriate resonance? What if it is done to save a loved one?<br /> <br /> Similarly, what if a Stormy (or Sparky) resonance mage wants to create a portal wonder, just like Dr. Strange's sling-ring (from MCU, with sparks separating outside from inside the portal), is it appropriate resonance? Which one(s) would be considered appropriate?<br /> <br /> What exactly should be analyzed: how the spell is done, how it looks like, why it is done, or any of those?<br />","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/3153-lord-revan","name":"Lord Revan","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar3153_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"May 2014"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"1419"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2025-08-14T14:54:10-07:00","id":1553187,"title":"","content":"The four main flavors are probably meant to correspond to the four Avatar essences, while the other resonances are meant to get more detailed.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/26432-jagnestormskull","name":"JagneStormskull","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar26432_2.png","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Sep 2020"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"73"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2025-08-14T20:57:51-07:00","id":1553194,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>JagneStormskull</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/mage-the-ascension/1553184-flavors-of-resonance?p=1553187#post1553187\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">The four main flavors are probably meant to correspond to the four Avatar essences, while the other resonances are meant to get more detailed.</div> </div> </div> </div>No, actually the goal of those flavors is ostensibly to depart from the Avatar Essences, as those are tackled to the sister rule of Synergy.<br /> <br /> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/3153-lord-revan\" style=\"background-image:url('https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5//avatar3153_2.jpeg');\" class=\"b-bbcode-user b-bbcode-user--has-avatar js-bbcode-user\" data-userid=\"3153\" data-vbnamecard=\"3153\">Lord Revan</a>, the answer includes a lot of &quot;do what you will&quot; with it, this system is meant to work by ST fiat instead of any consistent ruling. <br /> <br /> 1- Four &quot;categories&quot; of flavors with descriptors tackled on, yes, that's how it's supposed to work. There's no limit to the number of possible descriptors, it's all on &quot;feeling&quot;;<br /> <br /> 2- The four main Flavors actually matter very little, unless you want them to matter more. The descriptors are really the whole point. And if you don't know where to tackle something, just go by feeling, again;<br /> <br /> 3- All the rules that depend on it are optional. You can fully ignore them. The fluff is the most important part even if you actually use the rules;<br /> <br /> 4- Opposed and similar are likewise defined by... feeling, just feeling, nothing more. If you feel they're related, then they're related;<br /> <br /> 5- You should analyze whatever you feel like, with a few caveats. As a rule of thumb, the main factors are A) who's the Mage, B) emotional context and C) Resonance already attached to any materials and targets involved. But any detail you feel you should consider, you should consider, and any detail you feel you shouldn't, you shouldn't;<br /> <br /> 6- In the end, the real goal is to add &quot;flavor&quot; to Magic, from whatever source you deem relevant, so that Mages can recognize this flavor descriptively and justify looking for some things instead of others. The system is working as intended when the characters start to look for the Resonance in a given spell to identify its caster, and when they look for resonance to decide with what, where and when to cast their spells and to choose Tass, instead of accept any Tass and Node as adequate and call it a day.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/>#NothingAboutUsWithoutUs<br /> #AutismPride<br /> She/her pronouns","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":1}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/5826-monteparnas","name":"monteparnas","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar5826_2.png","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Apr 2015"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"6710"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2025-08-14T22:51:41-07:00","id":1553198,"title":"","content":"It can also help to remember that Resonance - while not super contentious like other topics - is a big divide between 1e/2e and Revised that M20 was trying to juggle. In 1e/2e Resonance was purely ST fiat material. The books talk about, and say it's important, and suggest some potential things it could matter for, but really it was purely about monteparnas's 6th point. Revised added some real rules to Resonance to make it more structured in the game (including the idea of broad categories of Resonance that could have infinite potential variations of in a specific instance). There was still a lot of ST rules calls (esp. regarding the Resonance of non-mage elements of the setting) in Revised, but M20 is still trying to make something that has Revised's more in-depth approach with the flexibility of 1e/2e.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":2}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/617-heavy-arms","name":"Heavy Arms","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar617_2.jpeg","title":"Banned","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"12634"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2025-08-15T10:42:38-07:00","id":1553206,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>monteparnas</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/mage-the-ascension/1553184-flavors-of-resonance?p=1553194#post1553194\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">6- In the end, the real goal is to add &quot;flavor&quot; to Magic, from whatever source you deem relevant, so that Mages can recognize this flavor descriptively and justify looking for some things instead of others. The system is working as intended when the characters start to look for the Resonance in a given spell to identify its caster, and when they look for resonance to decide with what, where and when to cast their spells and to choose Tass, instead of accept any Tass and Node as adequate and call it a day.</div> </div> </div> </div>Personally, I like Resonance for that exact reason. It adds customization and different flavors to magick. And makes sense, since magick foci can be very different, why would all magick look (and feel) the same?<br /> <br /> I just don't like how they tell you it's optional rule-wise and then there are rules that pretty much depend on it. If you don't adopt Resonance optional rule, then awareness kinda lose its power. The modifiers are gone. Crafting wonders are hard. And Prime also loses a bit of its utility.<br /> <br /> Since there doesn't seem to be an objective answer, how do you (or your table) work with it?<br /> How would you react to my examples in op?<br />","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":1}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/3153-lord-revan","name":"Lord Revan","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar3153_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"May 2014"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"1419"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2025-08-15T12:23:41-07:00","id":1553209,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Lord Revan</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/mage-the-ascension/1553184-flavors-of-resonance?p=1553206#post1553206\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">I just don't like how they tell you it's optional rule-wise and then there are rules that pretty much depend on it. If you don't adopt Resonance optional rule, then awareness kinda lose its power. The modifiers are gone. Crafting wonders are hard. And Prime also loses a bit of its utility.<br /></div> </div> </div> </div>If you don't adopt the rules, Resonance still has the capacity to identify a spell's caster. But part of the &quot;feeling&quot; involved is that in theory you should apply to Resonance whatever effect you want, the opposite of not having any effect at all. Just make shit up, have some effects be more or less powerful, more or less precise, have the Mage lose control of an effect, or a Wonder manifest a host of unexpected powers and problems.<br /> <br /> And if you think this is hard and silly... well, that'as why <i>Revised</i> tried to tackle some rules. Resonance is the most free-form rule in Mage for better or worse, and it definitely isn't without controversy even before <i>Revised</i>.<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Lord Revan</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/mage-the-ascension/1553184-flavors-of-resonance?p=1553206#post1553206\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">Since there doesn't seem to be an objective answer, how do you (or your table) work with it?<br /> How would you react to my examples in op?</div> </div> </div> </div>I usually focus on the intent of the spell rather than its mechanical effect, also thinking about how that &quot;flavor&quot; would descriptively alter the effect. A &quot;malicious&quot; Fireball seems to me like something that would cause more uncomfortable damage than be more destructive <i>per se</i>, so I would probably have this resonance be irrelevant beyond how I describe the effect. Something similar for the Resonance related to saving a loved one.<br /> <br /> Overall I treat most Resonance as neutral. They affect the result's description to the degree they're relevant (most sudden emotion-fueled Resonance isn't that big), but that's about it. I reserve the rules for A) when it's really relevant to the scene or B) when the PCs actively go after it.<br /> <br /> Most of the time Resonance doesn't really play a big role, otherwise it would be more of a distraction than anything.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/>#NothingAboutUsWithoutUs<br /> #AutismPride<br /> She/her pronouns","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/5826-monteparnas","name":"monteparnas","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar5826_2.png","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Apr 2015"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"6710"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2025-08-16T12:00:16-07:00","id":1553221,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>monteparnas</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/mage-the-ascension/1553184-flavors-of-resonance?p=1553194#post1553194\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">No, actually the goal of those flavors is ostensibly to depart from the Avatar Essences, as those are tackled to the sister rule of Synergy.</div> </div> </div> </div>Synergy flavors correspond to the metaphysical trinity: Static, Dynamic, and Entropic; and they operate on a paradigm of the world influencing the mage. Resonance flavors correspond to the Avatar Essences: Devotional is Questing, Temperamental is Dynamic, Stabilizing is Pattern, and Elemental is Primordial. And the paradigm for Resonance is the mage influencing the world. <br /> <br /> There's no mechanics linking the Essences and the Resonances, though. My own approach is that the sorts of activities that, say, a Pattern Avatar seeks to promote tends to be the kinds of things that would lead to Stabilizing Resonance; but that the Resonance that the mage develops is based on the mage's actions, not the Avatar's preferences. <br /> <br /> MRev attempted to connect the metaphysical trinity to the Avatar Essences; and while there are two strong connections (&quot;Dynamic&quot; exists in both sets, and &quot;Static&quot; and &quot;Pattern&quot; are easy to correlate), the rest is weak at best (MRev tried to connect &quot;Entropic&quot; to &quot;Primordial&quot; despite the two having concepts largely opposed to one another, and argued that &quot;Questing&quot; is about finding balance among Stasis, Dynamism, and Entropy). This attempt to try to force the Avatar Essences into the metaphysical trinity was very much a case of trying to pound a square peg into a triangular hole. <br /> <br /> It's why I eventually abandoned the three-fold model of Quiet that MRev introduced and instead adopted a &quot;Resonance influences your Quiet&quot; model: someone who has a strong Temperamental Resonance is going to find his bouts of Quiet connected to that emotion; if he has a strong Devotional Resonance, then the imagery surrounding that belief system will tend to manifest in Quiet. Elemental Resonance will end up with everything the character sees overlaid by that Element. And Stabilizing Resonance causes Quiets to be structured affairs according to the specifics of the Stabilizing Resonance. <br /> <br /> To the extent that the threefold model is still in play, it's that the effects of Quiet tend to manifest in three broad ways: seeing things that aren't there (Delusion), <i>not</i> seeing things that <i>are</i> there (Denial), and seeing what's there, but not as it is (Distortion). But these aren't mutually exclusive manifestations of Quiet; a given bout of Quiet can feature all three. <br /> <br /> Jhor is an interesting case study: MRev miscategorized it as a type of Quiet; M20 calls it a specific Elemental Resonance, where the element in question is Death. What M20 calls Morbidity is, in my view, a Quiet influenced by Elemental (Death) Resonance that manifests primarily as Distortions, coloring everything you see with the pallor of death and decay. <br /> <br /> Meanwhile, Synergy tends to influence Quiet less and Paradox more.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/><div style=\"margin-left:40px\"> <div align=\"center\">My hacks: <a href=\"http://tinyurl.com/dwtrin\" target=\"_blank\"><span style=\"color:#0000FF\">Trinity Continuum</span></a>, <a href=\"http://tinyurl.com/dwscion\" target=\"_blank\"><span style=\"color:#008000\">Scion</span></a>, <a href=\"http://tinyurl.com/dwExalt\" target=\"_blank\"><span style=\"color:#DAA520\">Exalted</span></a>, <a href=\"http://tinyurl.com/dwcwod\" target=\"_blank\"><span style=\"color:#800000\">World of Darkness</span></a>, <a href=\"http://tinyurl.com/dwnwod\" target=\"_blank\"><span style=\"color:#FF0000\">Chronicles of Darkness</span></a></div> </div>","edited":{"userid":421,"user_link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/421-dataweaver","user":"Dataweaver","date":". 08-17-2025, 03:38 AM"},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":2}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/421-dataweaver","name":"Dataweaver","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar421_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"8420"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2025-08-22T09:40:44-07:00","id":1553375,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>monteparnas</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/mage-the-ascension/1553184-flavors-of-resonance?p=1553209#post1553209\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">I usually focus on the intent of the spell rather than its mechanical effect, also thinking about how that &quot;flavor&quot; would descriptively alter the effect. A &quot;malicious&quot; Fireball seems to me like something that would cause more uncomfortable damage than be more destructive <i>per se</i>, so I would probably have this resonance be irrelevant beyond how I describe the effect. Something similar for the Resonance related to saving a loved one.</div> </div> </div> </div>I see. M20 also seem to focus on spells intents, but when you have a non-emotion Resonance, like fiery or warlike, then the intention kinda doesn't matter?<br /> <br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>monteparnas</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/mage-the-ascension/1553184-flavors-of-resonance?p=1553209#post1553209\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">Overall I treat most Resonance as neutral. They affect the result's description to the degree they're relevant (most sudden emotion-fueled Resonance isn't that big), but that's about it. I reserve the rules for A) when it's really relevant to the scene or B) when the PCs actively go after it.<br /> <br /> Most of the time Resonance doesn't really play a big role, otherwise it would be more of a distraction than anything.</div> </div> </div> </div>Yeah, describing the resonance of every single effect would be tiresome. Also, I have the impression that it's kinda simple for players to get the modifier bonus for matching resonance, all is needed is a bit of creativity and mental gym<br />","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/3153-lord-revan","name":"Lord Revan","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar3153_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"May 2014"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"1419"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2025-08-22T14:58:15-07:00","id":1553386,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Lord Revan</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/mage-the-ascension/1553184-flavors-of-resonance?p=1553375#post1553375\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">Also, I have the impression that it's kinda simple for players to get the modifier bonus for matching resonance, all is needed is a bit of creativity and mental gym</div> </div> </div> </div>That's the point.<br /> <br /> By giving a bonus for matching resonance, you make the players care and engage with this aspect of the game. And that's it. As long as they play along, being a challenge is besides the point.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/>#NothingAboutUsWithoutUs<br /> #AutismPride<br /> She/her pronouns","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/5826-monteparnas","name":"monteparnas","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar5826_2.png","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Apr 2015"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"6710"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2025-08-26T11:06:57-07:00","id":1553463,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>monteparnas</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/mage-the-ascension/1553184-flavors-of-resonance?p=1553386#post1553386\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\"><br /> That's the point.<br /> <br /> By giving a bonus for matching resonance, you make the players care and engage with this aspect of the game. And that's it. As long as they play along, being a challenge is besides the point.</div> </div> </div> </div>Yeah, I guess you're right. The bonuses are there to be used, and if the player is taking the effort to explain the effect into it, then I think it's fair.<br /> <br /> <br /> Regarding wonder creation and Tass resonance (I use FbDF), does the resonance of the Tass require to match the resonance of the effect or the object? Or any of them?<br /> <br /> For example, if you want to make a candle Charm and you have candles with Elemental Fiery resonance and you're fueling the Charms with obsidian with fiery Tass, to enchant them with a Healing effect (Life, which doesn'tinitially have fiery resonance, at least without a good mental gym), does it require Prime 2 or Prime 3?","signature":"","edited":{"userid":3153,"user_link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/3153-lord-revan","user":"Lord Revan","date":". 08-26-2025, 02:16 PM"},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/3153-lord-revan","name":"Lord Revan","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar3153_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"May 2014"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"1419"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2025-08-26T11:49:30-07:00","id":1553468,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Lord Revan</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/mage-the-ascension/1553184-flavors-of-resonance?p=1553463#post1553463\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\"><br /> Regarding wonder creation and Tass resonance (I use FbDF), does the resonance of the Tass require to match the resonance of the effect or the object? Or any of them?</div> </div> </div> </div>To be precise, the idea is for the Resonance in this case to match (or not) the <i>project</i>. That is, the thematic aspects of your vision of the finished Wonder. Usually that means the effect has more say than the object, but the symbolism of the finished product does, too.<br /> <br /> As an example, lets say you want to make those charms that are ginger candies that make you breath fire. It may be that Resonance related to sweets or food works, and it is more likely that a Resonance related to fire works, but it could be that the best choices are Resonance related to spices specifically, or to dragons, or even to some breathing and meditation techniques, because &quot;breathing fire&quot; has a strong connection with those ideas.<br /> <br /> But your best bet is probably to let all of that apply, and slightly modify the details of the Wonder based on the Resonance the player effectively chose. Wonders are unpredictable, and part of their effects are defined by the ST, not the creator, even when everything goes just fine. Let that guide your decisions. The candy made with Sweet Resonance may be more pleasant to eat and leave a sweet air in the place, or even make you slightly more pleasant to be around between eating and expending the effect. The candy made with Fiery Resonance may heat you a little and even have your throat glow bright orange, and so on.<br /> <br /> Of course, this is more relevant for permanent Wonders. The more powerful the Wonder, the more it should show the effects of the Resonance.<br /> <br /> For the level of Prime, if I understood correctly your question, it has nothing to do with Resonance. In this case you would use Prime 2, because while the effect is of Life and it will target Life patterns, you're enchanting a candle.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/>#NothingAboutUsWithoutUs<br /> #AutismPride<br /> She/her pronouns","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/5826-monteparnas","name":"monteparnas","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar5826_2.png","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Apr 2015"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"6710"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2025-09-01T15:08:03-07:00","id":1553583,"title":"","content":"<a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/5826-monteparnas\" style=\"background-image:url('https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5//avatar5826_2.png');\" class=\"b-bbcode-user b-bbcode-user--has-avatar js-bbcode-user\" data-userid=\"5826\" data-vbnamecard=\"5826\">monteparnas</a> <br /> I see. Your ginger example kinda matches both because ginger is kinda a fiery thing that can easily have that kind of Resonance and the effect is to breath fire. What if those same ginger candies (fiery Resonance) would heal instead? I'd say that maybe the effect should also include some fiery aspect, even if just for fluff, like the user gets engulfed in cold flames while healing, idk...<br /> <br /> Regarding the cost, FbDF have Prime 3 making Charms with Tass from any Resonance and Prime 2 making Charms using appropriate Resonance Tass. M20 turned that into modiffiers to the crafting rolls and use Prime 2 for all charms and kinds of Tass Resonance, iirc.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/3153-lord-revan","name":"Lord Revan","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar3153_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"May 2014"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"1419"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2025-09-01T18:05:10-07:00","id":1553585,"title":"","content":"<a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/3153-lord-revan\" style=\"background-image:url('https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5//avatar3153_2.jpeg');\" class=\"b-bbcode-user b-bbcode-user--has-avatar js-bbcode-user\" data-userid=\"3153\" data-vbnamecard=\"3153\">Lord Revan</a><br /> My point is that the best approach may be to let any rationale the character comes with to justify that Resonance fly, but have it affect the final effect.<br /> <br /> But that said, maybe the fiery Resonance wouldn't be the best fit? Even if it gives the bonus on crafting, the effects of such a Resonance may not be the best for every healing effect (although it may fit some). In the end, it's not a science.<br /> <br /> FbDF is its own thing. I personally use the rules in M20, as they're already based on it and are simpler. But it doesn't change things much, as long as the player at least tries to find adequate Tass, it should be doable with Prime 2. The point is less to make strict rules on what is or isn't appropriate Resonance and more to have the PCs putting in the effort to acquire it.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/>#NothingAboutUsWithoutUs<br /> #AutismPride<br /> She/her pronouns","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/5826-monteparnas","name":"monteparnas","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar5826_2.png","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Apr 2015"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"6710"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2025-09-02T10:13:19-07:00","id":1553590,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>monteparnas</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/mage-the-ascension/1553184-flavors-of-resonance?p=1553585#post1553585\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\"><a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/3153-lord-revan\" style=\"background-image:url('https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5//avatar3153_2.jpeg');\" class=\"b-bbcode-user b-bbcode-user--has-avatar js-bbcode-user\" data-userid=\"3153\" data-vbnamecard=\"3153\">Lord Revan</a><br /> My point is that the best approach may be to let any rationale the character comes with to justify that Resonance fly, but have it affect the final effect.<br /> <br /> But that said, maybe the fiery Resonance wouldn't be the best fit? Even if it gives the bonus on crafting, the effects of such a Resonance may not be the best for every healing effect (although it may fit some). In the end, it's not a science.<br /> <br /> FbDF is its own thing. I personally use the rules in M20, as they're already based on it and are simpler. But it doesn't change things much, as long as the player at least tries to find adequate Tass, it should be doable with Prime 2. The point is less to make strict rules on what is or isn't appropriate Resonance and more to have the PCs putting in the effort to acquire it.</div> </div> </div> </div>I agree. Yeah, fiery is definitely not the best resonance for healing, but it might be the only one available, and a creative player might try (and succeed) to fit into. Especially if the effect fluff affects the resonance as wel las the effect per se.<br /> <br /> I used M20/Bos system, but it is just so confusing when it comes to custom wonders that I was forced to change that. I mean regarding costs in chargen, it's a complete mess, very ambiguous and both the possible interpretations are terrible.They end up being ridiculous expensive or too cheap. And as I don't like to mix different systems for the same subject, I'd rather use all of M20 or all of FbDF rules for wonders and familiars. FbDF is extremely better at explaining and makes more sense, at least to me...","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/3153-lord-revan","name":"Lord Revan","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar3153_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"May 2014"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"1419"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2025-11-05T02:44:09-08:00","id":1555105,"title":"","content":"Here\u2019s my take, for what it is worth<br /> <br /> I see the resonances as described in BoS to relate to the motivations of the mage, not specific goals so much as the overarching reason as to why they act as they do. But the resonance is not something that guides the mage like Nature does, but is instead a reflection of the mage\u2019s own desires. Basically if what the mage wants is likened to a newly brewed coffee, then the resonance is the steam coming off the coffee, with the sent of whatever flavours the coffee contains. Change the coffee, then you change the flavours<br /> <br /> devotional resonances are for mages that have a strong core to refer back to. They either believe in a cause or have great conviction in a methodology<br /> <br /> elemental resonances are for mages that have strong primal roots, theirs is more about instinct and gut feeling than the reasoned thoughts of devotional, mages that are adverse to all things influenced by technology may also have this resonance to some degree<br /> <br /> stablizing resonances are for mages that really would rather chill or keep things going, they either don\u2019t want to rock the boat, or rather the boat was on a gentle calm sea. they may build things, but unlike devotional, they are not motivated to build things for a cause, but for the process itself, or to maintain some form of conservation effort<br /> <br /> Temperamental resonance are for mages who are very reactionary and emotion lead. They may be very attuned to one particular emotion or very flighty, but the reason for their actions is often in response to a burst of emotional experience. Unlike elemental, their responses are not measured or based on a sense of awareness, but on a desire to deal with what they are feeling right now in the moment.<br /> <br /> <br /> i like to have these resonances reflect the decisions my mages make, and have them influence their magic depending on the reason they are casting as well as the way they go about casting<br /> <br /> <br /> to answer your first question, this is how I might deal with the flavours you mentioned<br /> <br /> Sorrowful:- Temperamental<br /> <br /> Joyous:- temperamental if it is a manic joy, if it is a more subdued sunset type of joy, then Stabilizing<br /> <br /> Warlike:- Devotional if they subscribe to gods of war or the machinery of battle as an ideal, Elemental if war is simply a response to their primordial urges for conflict<br /> <br /> <br /> Depressing:- Temperamental. I could see an argument for it being a good flavour for Stabilising too, but such a mage would need to paradoxically be comfortable with their depression, which is sort of an oxymoron<br /> <br /> Tempestuous:- temperamental, assuming you mean their emotions are all over the place<br /> <br /> <br /> Calm:- stabilizing<br /> <br /> Savage:- Elemental<br /> <br /> Tragic:- temperamental<br /> <br /> Malicious:- Elemental if they subscribe to the idea that cruelty is at the heart of human desire, but Temperamental if they instead are simply a vicious individual<br /> <br /> Treacherous:- same with Malicious, though ai would lean more towards Temperamental with that one. If however they are a Count of Monte Cristo type character, then Devotional would be a better fit <br /> <br /> Feral:- Elemental<br /> <br /> Prosperous:- Devotional, though an argument can be made for stabilizing if the character just wanted everyone to vibe<br /> <br /> Greedy:- temperamental<br /> <br /> Accultured:- stabilising if aculturality for a sense of amorphosing cultures together, elemental is harkening to a time before man invented its cultures.<br /> <br /> Honorable:- devotional<br /> <br /> Sneaky:- Elemental for those that harken back to a time where being deceptive meant being alive, Stabilizing if sneakiness is a means to not be involved or be noticed<br /> <br /> Sinister:- Elemental, the time before man is often sinister to those who buy in to the lie of civility, or temperamental if one\u2019s sinister acts are done ins response to something they feel entitled to respond to<br /> <br /> Corrupting:- Elemental","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/><a href=\"http://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/mage-the-ascension/959615-chronicle-report-keepers-of-the-wyck\" target=\"_blank\">Keepers of the Wyck: A Chronicle I'm running</a> FINALE: Chapter 39: Green Fairy","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/12193-saikou","name":"Saikou","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar12193_2.png","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Jul 2016"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"1406"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2025-11-30T07:32:08-08:00","id":1555978,"title":"","content":"<a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/12193-saikou\" style=\"background-image:url('https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5//avatar12193_2.png');\" class=\"b-bbcode-user b-bbcode-user--has-avatar js-bbcode-user\" data-userid=\"12193\" data-vbnamecard=\"12193\">Saikou</a> I understand that you do &quot;fit&quot; the different resonance adjectives intobthe four flavors. If that's what we're supposed to do, I just don't understand why have so many adjectives in the first place. <br /> <br /> Also, what are your thoughts about the opposed/similar rule for wonder creation?","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/3153-lord-revan","name":"Lord Revan","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar3153_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"May 2014"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"1419"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2025-11-30T13:29:46-08:00","id":1555993,"title":"","content":"I don't think Saikou meant it in the way of &quot;how it's supposed to be&quot;. It's not supposed to be as much as it is one way of doing it.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/>#NothingAboutUsWithoutUs<br /> #AutismPride<br /> She/her pronouns","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/5826-monteparnas","name":"monteparnas","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar5826_2.png","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Apr 2015"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"6710"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2025-12-05T11:35:15-08:00","id":1556175,"title":"","content":"Bear in mind that while the concept of Resonance was in the game from the very beginning, the original implementation of it (and the one Brucato prefers) was the Echoes Flaw: the idea is that as you use your Magick in certain ways, you develop Echoes reflecting that.<br /> <br /> That was it. Yes, you had Avatar Essences; but they weren't about what kind of Resonance you develop. They were solely and exclusively your Avatar's &quot;Nature&quot;. Nothing more, nothing less.<br /> <br /> Then the Revised Edition came along and added Resonance dots to the character sheet. The stated purpose was to draw attention to Resonance; and the rules for what those dots represent and how (or even if) they are to be used was all over the place. MRev chose to have Resonance reflect the Metaphysical Trinity, giving you Static, Dynamic, and Entropic Resonances as broad categories of Resonance in which more specific types would be specified.<br /> <br /> It was also linked to the newly tripartate Quiet system, where Quiet now came in Static, Dynamic, and Entropic types: specifically, Clarity, Madness, and Jhor. It also aligned the three types of Resonance to the four Avatar Essences, noting how even the 1st Edition drew a connection between the Technocracy and Pattern, the Marauders and Dynamic, the Nephandi and Primordial.<br /> <br /> When Brucato came back to do the 20th Anniversary Edition, he had several critiques of MRev's Resonance system. Since the stated purpose of said edition was to mend the rift between fans of the Second and Revised Editions, he <i>didn't</i> simply jettison the MRev Resonance rules, but instead came up with an alternative to it that addressed his concerns while maintaining the same overall approach.<br /> <br /> First, the tripartate Resonances, which MRev envisioned as the world influencing the mage, got recast as Synergies, and decoupled them from the Avatar Essences while maintaining their coupling to the tripartate Quiet. Which he renamed as Denial, Madness, and Morbidity: the former because Clarity didn't properly convey the problematic nature of (a type of) Quiet, and the latter because 2e had presented Jhor as &quot;the Death Resonance&quot;, not a type of Quiet. More on this later. He <i>didn't</i> rename Madness, which is ironic given the subsequent push to see the term stricken from the record (see how <b>Mage: the Awakening</b> second edition renamed the first edition's Mad as the Rapt, and how it's now considered insensitive to speak about &quot;mad scientists&quot;). I sometimes wonder what an M20 Marauders sourcebook would have done in this regard.<br /> <br /> Second, he introduced a new Resonance system loosely based on the Essences, but <i>not</i> connected to the Metaphysical Trinity, nor to the tripartate model . That's where you get Stabilizing, Temperamental, Elemental, and Devotional. As I see it, their connection to the Essences are indirect: Avatars guide mages towards certain types of activities; and <i>if</i> the mages accept that guidance, the activities they engage in will produce Resonances that align with the Avatar's Essence. But ultimately, the type of Resonance you develop depends on the actions you take, <i>not</i> on the Essence of your Avatar. He also downplayed the aforementioned alignments between the Essences and the Metaphysical Trinity, noting that all four Essences can be found among each faction in the War.<br /> <br /> Note that both Resonance and Synergy systems are entirely optional. <i>If</i> you want to put Resonance dots on the character sheet, there's a lot to be said for the M20 approach as the four flavors of Resonance can be understood on their own without necessarily having to refer to the organizing principle that they're based on (the Avatar Essences). Furthermore, it doesn't try to force a square peg into a triangular hole: while associations can be drawn between the Pattern Essence and Stasis and between the Dynamic Essence and Dynamism, &quot;Primordial&quot; either isn't a very good name for what it is, or it isn't a particularly good fit for Entropy: by its common definition, &quot;Primordial&quot; echoes something more along the lines of the Garou from Werewolf or the Yang-aspected Kuei-Jin of Kindred of the East: something primitive and untamed; potentially violent but more life-oriented than death-touched: visceral like the Verbena rather than morbid like vampires, Yin aspected Kuei-Jin, or Euthanatoi.<br /> <br /> That works nicely with Elemental Resonances, which can be thought of as being similar to how Werewolf's spirits are associated with various concepts in reality: Elemental Resonances can be Earth/Air/Fire/Water/Wood/ etc.; but it can also be things like Machines, Trees, Money, Life, Death, etc. Which is why Jhor (the Death Resonance, remember) is an Elemental Resonance.<br /> <br /> Rather than being about maintaining a balance between the three legs of the Metaphysical Trinity, the Questing Avatar is about serving some sort of greater purpose. The stereotype for this would be the Celestial Chorus; but anyone who's &quot;on a mission&quot; and serving something greater than themselves can be compatible with a Questing Avatar.<br /> <br /> And that's why Questing Avatars are loosely affiliated with Devotional Resonances: the specific Resonance typically established the cause that you're pursuing.<br /> <br /> And that same logic also extends to the pairing of a Pattern Essence with a Stabilizing Resonance and the pairing of a Dynamic Essence with a Temperamental Resonance. Those two <i>do</i> tend to represent opposites of a sort, with Stabilizing Resonances tending to represent more is strength, fortitude, calm reasoning, rationality, <i>clarity</i>, and in general a tendency toward a cerebral mindset; while Temperamental Resonances are aligned more with emotional drives: love, hate, anger, fear, and so on. <hr /><br /> My own take is as follows:<ol class=\"decimal\"><li>Places and things have Resonance that operates like what M20 calls Synergy: it comes in Dynamism, Stasis, and Entropic flavors, with specific adjectives specializing the meaning. This kind of Resonance can be tapped into when working Magick, as a Magickal Modifier.</li> <li>By contrast, <i>people</i> have the four Resonance stats roughly corresponding to the Avatar Essences. <i>These</i> Resonances are the ones that result in Magickal workings having the mage's &quot;fingerprints&quot; all over them, allowing Magicks for sensing Resonance to identify who crafted an Effect and getting a rough sense of what they're like.</li> <li>A combination of a mage's Resonances and the Effects she creates ends up affecting the <s>Synergy</s>Resonance of the places and things that the Magick affects. This is <i>not</i> a one-to-one mapping between personal Resonance and the Synergy of a place or thing; in fact, the choice of Effect tends to have more to do with how a subject's Synergy is affected than the willworker's Resonance does.</li> <li>I ditch the tripartate Quiet model; though I borrow ideas from it. Quiet can manifest in three different ways: manifestation of things that aren't there (similar to the tripartate concept of Madness), suppression of things that <i>are</i> there (kind of like Denial), and coloring perceptions (similar to how Morbidity causes a mage to see everything through the lens of death and decay). The difference is that these three ways aren't mutually exclusive, and in fact tend to coexist in a single Quiet. And relevant to this discussion, your Resonance stats flavor your Quiets, influencing what you deny, what you manifest, and what you emphasize.</li> </ol><ol class=\"decimal\"><li>Someone with a powerful Devotional Resonance will tend to see everything through the lens of his favored cause, sometimes even seeing signs of it when it isn't there, or <i>not</i> seeing evidence that contradicts it. This is your fanatic, whether it be the Fundamentalist who won't shut up about how you need Jesus to save you, or the person who is the reason for the joke about vegans (&quot;how do you know if someone's a vegan? Don't worry; they'll tell you.&quot;)</li> <li>So-called Morbidity becomes &quot;merely&quot; a regular Quiet influenced by the Death Resonance, with hobgoblins taking the form of spectral entities, a pallor of death covering everything, and the more life-affirming elements of reality somehow being overlooked. Other Elemental Resonances would influence Quiet in a similar way, just featuring a different Element.</li> <li>Someone with a Stabilizing Resonance tends to deny elements of reality that don't fit into the models that he's constructed to explain reality, while becoming fixated on elements that <i>do</i> fit and sometimes even inventing evidence that reaffirms the model. Negation Men (described in <b>Book of Secrets</b>) are Marauders with a powerful Stabilizing Resonance that causes them to think that mundane reality is all that there is: think in terms of <b>Changeling</b>'s Banality.</li> <li>And someone with a strong Temperamental Resonance tends to see triggers everywhere, whether it not they're actually there. Mountains are made of molehills; anything that might soothe the mage is ignored. While in Quiet, they're in a perpetual state of outrage, or paranoia, or infatuation (see the anime concept of the yandere), etc. Whatever the emotion that the Temperamental Resonance is defined as, the Quiet mage takes it to an unhealthy extreme and is dominated by it.</li> </ol>","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/><div style=\"margin-left:40px\"> <div align=\"center\">My hacks: <a href=\"http://tinyurl.com/dwtrin\" target=\"_blank\"><span style=\"color:#0000FF\">Trinity Continuum</span></a>, <a href=\"http://tinyurl.com/dwscion\" target=\"_blank\"><span style=\"color:#008000\">Scion</span></a>, <a href=\"http://tinyurl.com/dwExalt\" target=\"_blank\"><span style=\"color:#DAA520\">Exalted</span></a>, <a href=\"http://tinyurl.com/dwcwod\" target=\"_blank\"><span style=\"color:#800000\">World of Darkness</span></a>, <a href=\"http://tinyurl.com/dwnwod\" target=\"_blank\"><span style=\"color:#FF0000\">Chronicles of Darkness</span></a></div> </div>","edited":{"userid":421,"user_link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/421-dataweaver","user":"Dataweaver","date":". 12-12-2025, 05:39 AM"},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":3}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/421-dataweaver","name":"Dataweaver","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar421_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"8420"}]},"thankedby":[]}]}